@{
    Layout = "~/Views/Shared/_Layout.cshtml";
}

<section class="py-4">
    <div class="container">
        <h1 class="mb-3">Chain-of-Thought (CoT) Prompting</h1>
        <p class="lead">Chain-of-Thought prompting encourages the model to think step-by-step. Use it when you need the model to show reasoning rather than jump directly to an answer.</p>

        <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4">When to use CoT</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Multi-step calculations or logic puzzles.</li>
                    <li>Explaining how a conclusion was reached.</li>
                    <li>Auditing the reasoning process for mistakes.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card shadow-sm border-0 mb-4">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4">Example Prompt</h2>
                <pre class="bg-light p-3 rounded"><code>System: You are a careful math tutor. Think step-by-step before giving the final answer.
User: If a factory makes 12 bolts every 5 minutes, how many bolts are produced in 2 hours?</code></pre>
                <p class="mb-0">Encourage the model to show its intermediate calculations before producing the final value.</p>
            </div>
        </div>

        <div class="card shadow-sm border-0">
            <div class="card-body">
                <h2 class="h4">Tips</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li>Explicitly ask for reasoning (“show your working” or “list the steps”).</li>
                    <li>Review the reasoning for hallucinations or arithmetic slips.</li>
                    <li>Combine with constraints if you only want the final answer in a specific format.</li>
                </ul>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</section>
